WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.860
Avionics power starts on a 28 volt bus.

2
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:08.340
One that carries 80 volt surges, and 100 volt spikes

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.940
Yet every rail on the board must still come up in order.

4
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.081
First, the surges stop at the door.

5
00:00:15.141 --> 00:00:19.201
The input stage clamps them, refuses a reversed supply,

6
00:00:19.201 --> 00:00:23.221
and shuts out any bus that drifts outside its range.

7
00:00:23.221 --> 00:00:27.695
Next, one buck brings the bus down, limiting current on every cycle,

8
00:00:27.695 --> 00:00:30.815
so a short downstream cannot run away.

9
00:00:30.815 --> 00:00:34.165
That one output then feeds four regulators.

10
00:00:34.165 --> 00:00:40.565
5 volts, 3.3, 1.8, and a last rail at 1.2 or 0.9.

11
00:00:40.565 --> 00:00:45.365
Each one ramps up gently, not all at once.

12
00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:48.324
Every rail reports back from inside a narrow window.

13
00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:52.444
Only then does the sequencer let the next one start.

14
00:00:52.444 --> 00:00:55.604
Inside the buck, current sense closes a fast inner loop.

15
00:00:56.684 --> 00:01:01.264
Whether it holds steady from minus 55 to 125 degrees is not

16
00:01:01.264 --> 00:01:02.164
yet shown.

17
00:01:02.193 --> 00:01:07.033
And because one particle strike must never reorder a rail,

18
00:01:07.293 --> 00:01:11.113
the sequencer's logic is hardened, and triple-voted.

19
00:01:11.113 --> 00:01:12.712
None of this is silicon yet.

20
00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:14.812
Every figure is a target.

21
00:01:14.812 --> 00:01:19.272
The surge clamp, the loop margins, the upset spacing.

22
00:01:19.272 --> 00:01:21.612
All of it must still be proven

23
00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:24.312
So bring us your bus profile.

24
00:01:24.312 --> 00:01:25.792
Bring your rail list.

25
00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:29.572
And we will tell you exactly what SKU-3 must prove.
